Restore Commands 

# CCDC Linux Restore Commands

Run as root from inside the backup directory.

## 1. System Configurations

```bash
tar --acls --xattrs --selinux -xpf etc.tar -C /
```

## 2. Databases

### MySQL
```bash
mysql -u root -p < mysql.sql
```

### MariaDB
```bash
mariadb -u root -p < mariadb.sql
```

### PostgreSQL
```bash
sudo -u postgres psql -X -d postgres < postgres.sql
```

## 3. Website and Application Files

```bash
for archive in www.tar opt.tar srv.tar; do [ -f "$archive" ] || continue; tar -xpf "$archive" -C / || break; done
```

## 4. Users, Passwords and Groups

```bash
tar -xpf users.tar -C /
```

## 5. SSH Configuration

```bash
tar -xpf ssh.tar -C /
sshd -t && systemctl reload sshd
```

## 6. Cron Jobs

View backed-up cron jobs:

```bash
tar -tf cron.tar
```

Remove newly created user cron jobs that were absent from the backup:

```bash
crontab -u USERNAME -r
```

Restore original cron jobs:

```bash
tar -xpf cron.tar -C /
restorecon -RF /var/spool/cron
systemctl restart crond
```

## 7. Firewall

### Firewalld
```bash
tar -xpf firewall.tar -C /
firewall-cmd --reload
```

### iptables
```bash
iptables-restore < iptables.rules
```

## 8. Systemd Services and Timers

```bash
tar -xpf systemd.tar -C /
systemctl daemon-reload
```

## 9. Home Directories and SSH Keys

```bash
tar --acls --xattrs --selinux -xpf home.tar -C /
```

## 10. Sudo Permissions

```bash
tar -xpf sudo.tar -C /
visudo -c
```

## Final Verification

```bash
sshd -t
nginx -t
systemctl --failed
ss -tulpn
```

## Important Notes

- Restore only trusted backups.
- Restoring /etc may revert passwords and security hardening.
- Restoring archives does not delete newly created malicious files.
- Database restoration may overwrite existing data.
- Use the VM console when restoring authentication or firewall configurations.
- A file-level restoration does not guarantee removal of attacker persistence.
